1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,920
then that outcome becomes sustainable.

2
00:00:02,920 --> 00:00:05,240
Right, again, as long as we're dealing with,

3
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:07,400
so with an infinite repeated gain,

4
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:09,240
and actually since this is two equilibria,

5
00:00:09,240 --> 00:00:11,880
even a finitely repeated gain in principle,

6
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:15,540
we can go and get to better equilibria using repeat.

7
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:19,080
So peace is certainly sustainable,

8
00:00:19,080 --> 00:00:21,480
but again, there's also the possibility

9
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:23,240
that countries will try to build up a reputation

10
00:00:23,240 --> 00:00:25,120
for being warlike.

11
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:26,360
So this possibility, a country will try

12
00:00:26,360 --> 00:00:27,560
to build up a reputation for being

13
00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:29,680
very military aggressive and even crazy.

14
00:00:29,680 --> 00:00:32,240
It's kind of the North Korean strategy, right?

15
00:00:32,240 --> 00:00:33,880
It's just like, we don't care.

16
00:00:33,880 --> 00:00:36,040
Now, at this point, it does seem a little hard

17
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:37,400
to believe that the people in charge

18
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:39,140
don't care if they live or die,

19
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:41,560
because they've been living in luxury

20
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:44,140
for many decades, third generation.

21
00:00:44,140 --> 00:00:46,600
So it looks like they are there for the long haul.

22
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:48,160
So they're probably not as crazy

23
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:50,920
as they seem to outsiders, right?

24
00:00:50,920 --> 00:00:54,560
And so at least they are clever psychopaths

25
00:00:54,560 --> 00:00:58,480
rather than raving psychopaths.

26
00:00:58,480 --> 00:01:00,960
So the kind of people who will kill

27
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:02,880
totally defenseless people without mercy,

28
00:01:02,880 --> 00:01:05,040
but if they're worthy,

29
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:06,640
you will go and do something back to them,

30
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:08,360
then it's a different story.

31
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:13,120
Yeah, so that is another possibility, right?

32
00:01:13,120 --> 00:01:14,980
That these countries might try to be known

33
00:01:14,980 --> 00:01:17,760
for being very aggressive, so do not mess with us.

34
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:19,800
And of course, this could lead to wars

35
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:22,000
that seems really pointless,

36
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:24,320
where people go and have millions of people die

37
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:26,200
for a very small objective.

38
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:27,960
And again, there could be,

39
00:01:27,960 --> 00:01:29,240
again, it doesn't mean there is,

40
00:01:29,240 --> 00:01:31,440
but could be the underlying objective

41
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:33,680
of we want our reputation to be great.

42
00:01:33,680 --> 00:01:35,240
And you might even say the very fact

43
00:01:35,240 --> 00:01:36,640
we're willing to lose a lot of people

44
00:01:36,640 --> 00:01:38,400
for something that's inconsequential

45
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:40,240
shows how tough we are,

46
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:43,040
shows how little we value our own lives.

47
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:44,240
So do you wanna mess with people

48
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:46,380
who value their own lives so little?

49
00:01:46,380 --> 00:01:47,600
So when you go and read stories

50
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:49,640
about India and Pakistan fighting over

51
00:01:49,640 --> 00:01:52,440
an uninhabited mountaintop in the Himalayas somewhere,

52
00:01:53,320 --> 00:01:56,400
one explanation, again, not necessarily the best one,

53
00:01:56,400 --> 00:01:59,000
but one explanation is they're both trying to show

54
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:01,000
we're really tough and we will go

55
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:02,780
and we'll spend a lot of blood

56
00:02:02,780 --> 00:02:04,460
for something that doesn't even matter.

57
00:02:04,460 --> 00:02:07,800
So imagine what we would do for something that did matter.

58
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:09,880
All right, so there is that.

59
00:02:11,140 --> 00:02:12,880
All right, now, just to have a little digression

60
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:14,420
into history, thought, and philosophy.

61
00:02:14,420 --> 00:02:18,080
So how many people ever read Thomas Hobbes' Leviathan?

62
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:19,360
All right, and remember,

63
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:22,420
what are the five bad things about the state of nature?

64
00:02:22,420 --> 00:02:26,700
Solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short, yes.

65
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:31,700
Like the five dwarves of the Hobbesian state of nature.

66
00:02:32,300 --> 00:02:35,460
Solitary, yes, poor, solitary, nasty, brutish, and short.

67
00:02:35,460 --> 00:02:38,020
All right, now, if you go back and read

68
00:02:38,020 --> 00:02:41,340
exactly what Hobbes' argument is,

69
00:02:41,340 --> 00:02:45,100
it's very striking once you understand some game theory

70
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:47,420
because there's an overpowering temptation

71
00:02:47,420 --> 00:02:49,780
that people who know game theory have

72
00:02:49,780 --> 00:02:52,860
to interpret Thomas Hobbes as if he was right.

73
00:02:52,860 --> 00:02:55,580
But if you actually read his own words,

74
00:02:55,580 --> 00:02:57,540
I maintain that you will go and say,

75
00:02:57,540 --> 00:02:59,940
this guy doesn't know what he's talking about.

76
00:02:59,940 --> 00:03:03,380
A very smart guy who just didn't even understand

77
00:03:03,380 --> 00:03:07,140
the basics of what we now understand based upon this class.

78
00:03:07,140 --> 00:03:08,300
Right, and why would I say this?

79
00:03:08,300 --> 00:03:09,740
Well, so go and read it.

80
00:03:09,740 --> 00:03:11,340
And again, don't read it with the presumption

81
00:03:11,340 --> 00:03:12,160
that it's right.

82
00:03:12,160 --> 00:03:14,220
Just read it, read the literal words

83
00:03:14,220 --> 00:03:17,500
in the most natural, ordinary interpretation.

84
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:20,060
So here's what I claim Hobbes literally says.

85
00:03:21,060 --> 00:03:23,140
He seems to literally say

86
00:03:23,140 --> 00:03:26,380
that war is a strictly dominant strategy.

87
00:03:26,380 --> 00:03:29,380
Right, and you're always better off fighting.

88
00:03:29,380 --> 00:03:31,300
Why are you always better off fighting?

89
00:03:31,300 --> 00:03:32,900
Yeah, actually, it's a story.

90
00:03:32,900 --> 00:03:34,020
And it says war.

91
00:03:34,020 --> 00:03:36,100
So either the other side was gonna fight anyway,

92
00:03:36,100 --> 00:03:37,740
in which case, prepared.

93
00:03:37,740 --> 00:03:41,020
Or you went, or you couldn't be sure.

94
00:03:41,020 --> 00:03:42,140
And if you can't be sure,

95
00:03:42,140 --> 00:03:44,300
then you should just go and kill people, right?

96
00:03:44,300 --> 00:03:45,580
That makes perfect sense.

97
00:03:45,580 --> 00:03:48,620
Right, and again, if you read it,

98
00:03:48,620 --> 00:03:51,740
he does describe that it will degenerate

99
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:53,380
into universal warfare.

100
00:03:53,380 --> 00:03:57,500
But the idea that there is any downside at all of fighting

101
00:03:57,500 --> 00:03:59,680
seems completely outside of his thinking.

102
00:04:00,860 --> 00:04:03,120
The idea that you might go and get killed

103
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:04,540
because you picked a fight with someone

104
00:04:04,540 --> 00:04:08,260
that wasn't gonna fight you, totally absent.

105
00:04:08,260 --> 00:04:10,340
The idea that you might have just peacefully cooperated

106
00:04:10,340 --> 00:04:12,340
with someone, totally absent.

107
00:04:12,340 --> 00:04:14,880
Instead, there is this very strong assumption

108
00:04:14,880 --> 00:04:16,940
that whoever fights wins.

109
00:04:16,940 --> 00:04:19,140
And even if both sides are fighting,

110
00:04:19,140 --> 00:04:24,140
better to come and fight than to just be sitting there.

111
00:04:26,120 --> 00:04:28,540
So either the other person is not gonna fight,

112
00:04:28,540 --> 00:04:30,580
in which case, hit him over the head with a club.

113
00:04:30,580 --> 00:04:32,060
And or if the guy's got a club,

114
00:04:32,060 --> 00:04:33,940
hit him over the head with a club.

115
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:35,980
Right, which, the way I get it,

116
00:04:35,980 --> 00:04:37,340
remember, he is talking about,

117
00:04:37,340 --> 00:04:39,620
yeah, he's specifically talking about

118
00:04:39,620 --> 00:04:41,500
two random individuals beating each other

119
00:04:41,500 --> 00:04:42,660
in the state of nature.

120
00:04:42,660 --> 00:04:45,900
All right, now just think about this.

121
00:04:45,900 --> 00:04:49,780
Suppose that you are on a desert island all by yourself

122
00:04:49,780 --> 00:04:51,900
and another person washes up.

123
00:04:51,900 --> 00:04:54,140
How, what would have to happen

124
00:04:54,140 --> 00:04:56,060
before you would consider it a good strategy

125
00:04:56,060 --> 00:04:57,420
to go and murder that person

126
00:04:57,420 --> 00:04:59,580
before they are able to go and cough the water

127
00:04:59,580 --> 00:05:00,620
up out of their lungs?

128
00:05:00,620 --> 00:05:02,020
How big are they?

129
00:05:02,020 --> 00:05:04,220
That is a consideration.

130
00:05:04,220 --> 00:05:05,780
All right, so suppose the person's like

131
00:05:05,780 --> 00:05:07,220
two feet bigger than you.

132
00:05:07,220 --> 00:05:09,620
Are you still inclined to try to murder that person

133
00:05:09,620 --> 00:05:10,980
when you're all alone by yourself?

134
00:05:10,980 --> 00:05:11,980
Probably not.

135
00:05:11,980 --> 00:05:13,140
Yes.

136
00:05:13,140 --> 00:05:17,220
And see, again, that seems like a very poor strategy.

137
00:05:17,220 --> 00:05:19,340
Again, not that, well, how do you know

138
00:05:19,340 --> 00:05:20,500
he isn't a Charles Manson?

139
00:05:20,500 --> 00:05:22,540
I'm like a seven foot tall Charles Manson.

140
00:05:22,540 --> 00:05:23,700
All right, I don't.

141
00:05:23,700 --> 00:05:26,260
But it seems unlikely.

142
00:05:26,260 --> 00:05:28,940
And if I do, and if he wasn't Charles Manson,

143
00:05:28,940 --> 00:05:30,660
I murdered him, then I have to be,

144
00:05:30,660 --> 00:05:33,400
I have to continue being alone by myself.

145
00:05:33,400 --> 00:05:34,740
I'm like really lonely.

146
00:05:34,740 --> 00:05:36,100
I have no one to help me with anything.

147
00:05:36,100 --> 00:05:38,540
If I ever get sick, there's no one to bail me out.

148
00:05:38,540 --> 00:05:41,060
I'm in a terrible situation.

149
00:05:41,060 --> 00:05:43,780
And obviously, no one would think this

150
00:05:43,780 --> 00:05:45,540
if it were opposite genders.

151
00:05:46,460 --> 00:05:47,460
So if we're opposite genders,

152
00:05:47,460 --> 00:05:49,020
there's a man on an island, a woman washes up,

153
00:05:49,020 --> 00:05:50,700
no one thinks that the obvious prediction

154
00:05:50,700 --> 00:05:53,020
is he immediately murders the person and shuts me out.

155
00:05:53,020 --> 00:05:54,820
Or vice versa.

156
00:05:54,820 --> 00:05:56,700
No one thinks that a woman sees a man

157
00:05:56,700 --> 00:05:57,820
and washes him off the shore.

158
00:05:57,820 --> 00:05:59,380
All right, better kill him.

159
00:05:59,380 --> 00:06:00,700
See, it's the dominant strategy

160
00:06:00,700 --> 00:06:02,260
according to Hobbes' Leviathan.

161
00:06:02,260 --> 00:06:05,540
And then when you start thinking like,

162
00:06:05,540 --> 00:06:07,420
how bad would the situation have to be

163
00:06:07,420 --> 00:06:10,740
before it would be plausible that you would predict

164
00:06:10,740 --> 00:06:12,620
that the person would go and murder the,

165
00:06:12,620 --> 00:06:13,700
like the person who's already there

166
00:06:13,700 --> 00:06:15,780
would immediately murder the newcomer.

167
00:06:15,780 --> 00:06:18,020
So maybe if it's World War II

168
00:06:18,020 --> 00:06:19,420
and you're an American on the island

169
00:06:19,420 --> 00:06:21,460
and a Japanese soldier washes up,

170
00:06:22,940 --> 00:06:24,940
right, maybe then you kill him.

171
00:06:24,940 --> 00:06:27,020
Although I say even that depends on how many days

172
00:06:27,020 --> 00:06:29,460
you've been alone on that island.

173
00:06:29,460 --> 00:06:31,580
Like a week, yeah, you probably will kill him,

174
00:06:31,580 --> 00:06:33,940
especially if you've already been in combat

175
00:06:33,940 --> 00:06:36,700
and you've seen some friends get killed by Japanese soldiers.

176
00:06:36,700 --> 00:06:40,700
Then murdering him before he does anything is plausible,

177
00:06:40,700 --> 00:06:42,700
but you've been alone for a year,

178
00:06:43,540 --> 00:06:45,980
how are you gonna murder this guy?

179
00:06:45,980 --> 00:06:48,300
And it starts to seem highly implausible

180
00:06:48,300 --> 00:06:50,420
that this would happen, right?

181
00:06:50,420 --> 00:06:53,580
And again, partly it may just be that

182
00:06:53,580 --> 00:06:55,580
there's some psychology that Hobbes is considering,

183
00:06:55,580 --> 00:06:57,700
but again, there's also the obvious one of,

184
00:06:57,700 --> 00:07:00,020
look, there's a chance the person's gonna try to kill me,

185
00:07:00,020 --> 00:07:02,060
but there's also enormous gains of trade

186
00:07:02,060 --> 00:07:04,180
when you're the only person around.

187
00:07:04,180 --> 00:07:07,620
And if I kill a person, yes, I won't risk anything,

188
00:07:07,620 --> 00:07:11,380
but I also will give up all the possible gains,

189
00:07:11,380 --> 00:07:14,460
which in expected value terms are enormous.

190
00:07:15,620 --> 00:07:19,300
So that's something very weird about the scenario.

191
00:07:20,380 --> 00:07:23,020
Right, and then on top of all this,

192
00:07:23,020 --> 00:07:26,020
there's something else that isn't quite reflected there,

193
00:07:26,020 --> 00:07:29,100
but suppose that you go and decide

194
00:07:29,100 --> 00:07:32,620
you're going to fight a stranger right now and you win.

195
00:07:32,620 --> 00:07:35,620
Does that mean that everything worked out well for you

196
00:07:35,620 --> 00:07:38,820
just because you kill another person in the fight?

197
00:07:38,820 --> 00:07:42,180
Well, very often you get injured, right?

198
00:07:42,180 --> 00:07:43,700
So suppose that in that case,

199
00:07:43,700 --> 00:07:46,260
even if all you care about is physical safety,

200
00:07:46,260 --> 00:07:47,700
there is a since possibility

201
00:07:47,700 --> 00:07:49,100
that when you fight another person,

202
00:07:49,100 --> 00:07:51,820
that even if you win, you will be injured

203
00:07:51,820 --> 00:07:54,580
and the loss of the injury will outweigh

204
00:07:54,580 --> 00:07:57,020
whatever risk that the other person posed to you.

205
00:07:58,260 --> 00:08:00,100
Right, in fact, you could get mortally wounded

206
00:08:00,100 --> 00:08:01,220
even though you won the fight.

207
00:08:01,220 --> 00:08:03,100
That's a very extreme case.

208
00:08:03,100 --> 00:08:04,620
But again, imagine that you go,

209
00:08:04,620 --> 00:08:06,580
you could go and lose a finger.

210
00:08:06,580 --> 00:08:08,140
Right, now like one finger lost.

211
00:08:08,140 --> 00:08:09,580
Like well, what exactly,

212
00:08:09,580 --> 00:08:11,020
and what was the other person gonna do to me

213
00:08:11,020 --> 00:08:13,940
that was so terrible that it was worth losing this finger?

214
00:08:17,020 --> 00:08:20,700
So, to me, this is like Hobbes' scenario

215
00:08:20,700 --> 00:08:23,340
was interesting because almost everyone who reads it

216
00:08:23,340 --> 00:08:26,580
acts like he is correctly describing what would occur.

217
00:08:26,580 --> 00:08:28,300
And again, he's just describing two people

218
00:08:28,300 --> 00:08:31,700
without any garment bumping into each other in the forest

219
00:08:31,700 --> 00:08:34,540
and he sure seems to claim that they will definitely go

220
00:08:34,540 --> 00:08:36,260
and fight to the death on sight.

221
00:08:37,340 --> 00:08:39,220
And that seems like a very strange prediction

222
00:08:39,220 --> 00:08:41,100
both for game theoretic terms,

223
00:08:41,100 --> 00:08:42,900
namely like you're ignoring these benefits

224
00:08:42,900 --> 00:08:45,620
and also you're ignoring the possibility

225
00:08:45,620 --> 00:08:47,660
that you've been injured during the fight.

226
00:08:47,660 --> 00:08:49,020
So, I mean like if you just said

227
00:08:49,020 --> 00:08:50,500
they will immediately run away from each other

228
00:08:50,500 --> 00:08:53,500
for fear of injury, that would make a bit more sense to me.

229
00:08:54,340 --> 00:08:55,500
Right, you know, at least,

230
00:08:55,500 --> 00:08:58,780
like you're just like, oh my God, run.

231
00:08:58,780 --> 00:09:04,780
And that actually, I think that is more common in, you know,

232
00:09:04,780 --> 00:09:08,100
so when there are unexpected meetings

233
00:09:08,100 --> 00:09:11,860
between people in different groups in isolated settings,

234
00:09:11,860 --> 00:09:14,660
very common they both run away in terror.

235
00:09:14,660 --> 00:09:16,900
But you're like, I don't know that person, he might kill me.

236
00:09:16,900 --> 00:09:18,860
But the reaction of, and therefore,

237
00:09:18,860 --> 00:09:20,740
let me go and immediately fight this person

238
00:09:20,740 --> 00:09:21,900
who might kill me.

239
00:09:21,900 --> 00:09:24,060
It's like, well, why don't you just try running away?

240
00:09:24,060 --> 00:09:25,820
Maybe that would work better.

241
00:09:25,820 --> 00:09:28,780
So, like doesn't seem to be in Hobbes either.

242
00:09:29,860 --> 00:09:34,100
All right, so anyway, when you think about a situation,

243
00:09:34,100 --> 00:09:36,340
the equilibrium that Hobbes describes

244
00:09:36,340 --> 00:09:37,500
sounds like prisoner's dilemma,

245
00:09:37,500 --> 00:09:41,820
the actual situation seems much more like the Hawk-Dove game.

246
00:09:41,820 --> 00:09:43,620
And again, then why isn't a guy as smart

247
00:09:43,620 --> 00:09:46,300
as Hobbes correctly solve the model?

248
00:09:46,300 --> 00:09:47,620
Well, there's no game theory at the time,

249
00:09:47,620 --> 00:09:49,460
so he's just working it through verbally.

250
00:09:49,460 --> 00:09:53,060
And if you know anything else about Hobbes,

251
00:09:53,060 --> 00:09:55,380
he has a very dark personality.

252
00:09:55,380 --> 00:09:58,820
So, and he did live through the English Civil War.

253
00:09:59,860 --> 00:10:01,660
So he did see a lot of horrible stuff,

254
00:10:01,660 --> 00:10:03,980
although I'd still say that if you step back

255
00:10:03,980 --> 00:10:05,140
and thought about what occurred

256
00:10:05,140 --> 00:10:07,140
during that horrible Civil War that he saw,

257
00:10:07,140 --> 00:10:08,340
did he actually see what happens

258
00:10:08,340 --> 00:10:09,820
when totally isolated individuals

259
00:10:09,820 --> 00:10:11,820
meet each other in the wilderness?

260
00:10:11,820 --> 00:10:14,220
No, he saw what happens when groups

261
00:10:14,220 --> 00:10:16,380
who developed a strong group identity

262
00:10:16,380 --> 00:10:20,020
and antipathy towards the outsider meet each other.

263
00:10:20,020 --> 00:10:22,380
So not about isolated individuals,

264
00:10:22,380 --> 00:10:24,300
but rather about what happened,

265
00:10:24,300 --> 00:10:26,460
a story about group psychology,

